    V[k] = alpha*v_mech_k + beta*V_odom[k]
So per component:
V[k] = α·(V[k-1] + dt·a[k]) + (1-α)·V_odom[k]
Rewrite it as predict + correct (the form that reveals the filter structure):
V[k] = V[k-1] + dt·a[k]  +  β·( V_odom[k] − (V[k-1] + dt·a[k]) )
                         └──── innovation e[k]: odometry minus mechanized prediction ────┘
The mechanized branch ∫a is a pure integrator ⇒ a high-pass path (passes fast content). The odometry term is a low-pass path. They meet at the corner frequency
τ = 1/(2π·f_c) ,   α = τ/(τ+dt) ,   β = dt/(τ+dt)
- high f_c ⇒ small τ ⇒ α small ⇒ odometry (LPF) dominates ⇒ slow content taken from wheels.
- low f_c ⇒ large τ ⇒ α→1 ⇒ mechanization (HPF) dominates.
At f_c = 5 Hz, dt = 0.002 s: τ ≈ 31.8 ms, α ≈ 0.94, β ≈ 0.06. Nominally the IMU path "wins" 94/6 — but that 94/6 number is misleading, as the next section shows.
