#2 What signals are actually available, and why a_measured ≠ a_body
Hardware-available signals (the real sensor contract):
Signal	Hardware
w1..w4	wheel encoders
ψ̇ (yaw)	gyroscope
f_meas (3-axis)	accelerometer
The core point — equivalence principle. An accelerometer does not measure inertial acceleration. It measures specific force — the contact/reaction force per unit mass:
f = a_inertial − g
Concretely: an accelerometer sitting still on a table reads +1 g, not 0. It cannot tell "I'm accelerating upward" from "gravity is pulling down" — they're physically indistinguishable to the sensor. So the raw reading is
f_body = a_body  +  R(ψ)·g_world        (modulo sign convention)
              └accel we want┘  └gravity projected into body axes┘
To recover the body acceleration we must subtract the gravity projection, which needs a correct ψ (from the gyro) and a known/level mount:
a_body = f_body − R(ψ)·g_world
Reasons a_measured therefore differs from true a_body:
1. Gravity contamination (dominant). Until you subtract R(ψ)·g, the reading is dominated by a ~9.81 m/s² term that swings with heading. Any error in ψ (gyro) or in assuming the mount is level leaks gravity straight into the a_x, a_y you feed the filter.
2. Mount tilt / misalignment (mount_tilt_deg = 1.0° placeholder in the audit) — rotates the sensed axes away from body axes, so even gravity-corrected a is mixed between channels.
3. Bias (accel_bias_std = 0.02 m/s² placeholder) — a constant offset. This is the source of mech_only drift: it integrates ⇒ velocity error grows linearly (the ~0.07 m/s/s the audit measured).
4. Noise (accel_noise_std = 0.05 m/s² placeholder) — high-frequency, averages down over integration but jitters the estimate.
5. Transport / Coriolis terms. The body acceleration we actually need is a_x = V̇x − ψ̇·Vy, a_y = V̇y + ψ̇·Vx — it depends on ψ̇. A wrong gyro rate makes the transport term wrong; that's exactly why comp_filter.py offers rot_ab2 (exact planar rotation) vs euler (linearised).
6. Vibration / structural chatter — LuGre stick-slip chatter sits above ~250 Hz (imu_observable.py note); the accelerometer sees it, but it isn't rigid-body motion.
7. Lever arm — if the IMU isn't at the CG, rotation induces centripetal specific-force components the rigid-body model ignores.
Why this matters for the gate: trusting mechanization during slip requires only that (a) ψ̇ from the gyro stays good — and the gyro is slip-independent, so it does — and (b) the gravity projection is removed correctly. The leftover error is just bias + noise, whose integral (drift) is bounded by the episode length. That is the whole reason suspending odometry during slip is viable: the IMU channels stay trustworthy while the wheel channel does not.
