In the stick / pre-sliding region Vpᵢ ≈ 0, so this law predicts F ≈ 0. But the true contact force is not zero — it's the elastic bristle restoring force −N·σ0·z, which holds the contact without slip and can be sizeable. The steady-state law throws away the stick component (it only models the sliding/gross-slip branch where F ∝ Vp). So in stick we feed r_roll a wrong, near-zero force ⇒ the balance looks violated when physically it's satisfied ⇒ ℒ_phys becomes a bad indicator exactly in the low-slip regime. Your diagnosis is exactly right.
The root cause: any force-based loss (roller balance or body wrench balance) inherits the LuGre model's inaccuracy in stick, because the force itself is inaccurate there.
Fix: regime-split physics loss (recommended)
The key realization: in stick, γ is not a force-driven dynamical variable — it's kinematically slaved to (V, w, geometry). Set the contact velocity to zero (pure rolling, no slip):
Vpᵢ_x = Vx − ψ̇(pyᵢ+DYᵢ) − wᵢR + γᵢ·[sδᵢ(Ra·cθ̃ᵢ−R) + DYᵢ·cδᵢ·sθ̃ᵢ] = 0
Solve for γᵢ (pure geometry + V_used + wᵢ, no forces):
γ_kin,ᵢ = [ ψ̇·(pyᵢ+DYᵢ) + wᵢ·R − Vx ]
          ───────────────────────────────────────
          [ sδᵢ(Ra·cθ̃ᵢ−R) + DYᵢ·cδᵢ·sθ̃ᵢ ]
So in stick we replace the force-based balance with a kinematic constraint γ̂ᵢ = γ_kin,ᵢ(V_used, wᵢ, geo). This is exact in stick and needs no LuGre forces, no GT. The full loss becomes a smooth blend on a slip gate gᵢ ∈ [0,1] (computed from the model's own |Vpᵢ| = |Vp(V_used, γ̂, wᵢ)| — sensor-derived, deployable):
ℒ_phys = Σᵢ [ (1−gᵢ)·(γ̂ᵢ − γ_kin,ᵢ)²   +   gᵢ·r_roll,ᵢ(V_used, γ̂, F_ss)² ]
                 └ STICK ──┘                └ SLIP ──┘
   gᵢ → 0 in stick (kinematic, force-free)      gᵢ → 1 in slip (torque balance, forces accurate)
Why this is the right resolution
- Valid in both regimes, GT-free. Stick uses a force-free kinematic constraint (accurate there); slip uses the torque balance where steady-state LuGre is accurate. The failure mode you spotted is gone.
- Serves "phys-dominated even for spin." In stick, γ = γ_kin(V,w) is a hard constraint — it fully identifies γ̂ from sensors alone (no supervised floor needed). In slip, r_roll is underdetermined (3 vs 4 wheels) and the supervised floor carries identification, as before. So physics does the heavy lifting in both regimes; the floor is only the disambiguator.
