1. Mamba is a learnable state observer, which is the correct inductive bias
The whole dataset is generated by a 39-D ODE. A state-space model h_t = A·h_{t-1} + B·x_t is the discrete-time cousin of exactly that — so an SSM is the principled function class to learn an observer of the slip subsystem (bristles, roller spin, wheel ω), not just a correlator on a snapshot. Critically:
- γ̂ and ΔV̂ depend on the history of slip, not the instantaneous sample — the LuGre bristle state z integrates over time (pre-sliding elastic memory). A flat MLP on one timestep can't hold that; Mamba's recurrent hidden state is the compressed temporal memory.
- Because the "PINN" part is only a soft loss (scalar ℒ_phys on γ̂, ΔV̂), Mamba never has to autodiff through the ODE or embed a solver. It stays a clean, efficient sequence model; physics is bolted on outside. That's far lighter than a true PINN (which bakes the dynamics residual into the graph) and is precisely why Mamba is viable here.
2. It fixes the original failure mode — persistent cross-window memory
Recall the session's origin: the fixed filter had ~34 ms memory and failed on 0.5–2.4 s slip episodes. The brief trains on w32 windows (~64 ms) — shorter than an episode. The win is that Mamba runs recurrently at deployment: the SSM state is updated O(1) per step and carries across windows indefinitely, so effective context is unbounded, not clamped to 64 ms.
windowed training (w32)  ──unrolled chunk──►   deployment: step-by-step,
                                                          state persists across the
                                                          0.5–2.4 s episode
That persistent state is what lets the estimate hold through a slip episode — the exact capability the 34 ms filter lacked. A Transformer/MLP bounded to the window would forget the episode context the same way the old filter did.
3. Selective (input-dependent) dynamics = a learned soft slip-gate
Mamba's A,B,C are input-dependent. So the way the state evolves — what it remembers vs. discards — depends on the current input. During slip the state can retain the bristle/slip history; in steady rolling it can relax. That's a data-driven, soft version of the slip-gate we designed earlier, learned end-to-end and complementary to ℒ_phys. (We still keep ℒ_phys as the hard correctness enforcer — the SSM memory is the capacity, the loss is the constraint.)
